{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791821333,"nonce":"gP0STycDqm8bFGZ1+P6sWszoAB0=","message":"SDK manual-delay audit (2026-10-12), current published b9eff9c. Searched TypeScript SDK, its generated GPU/window bindings, native SDK sources, adapters and wiki launch for timers, sleep, timeout waits, debouncing and throttling; traced the native-window render/initialization scheduling paths. No production files changed; source audit only.\n\nRelevant manual timers in src/native/window.ts:107-120,224-257:\n- GPU readiness calls #schedule(true), requesting setTimeout(...,0). requestFrame outside an active paint/tick does the same, replacing an existing slower timer. This yields to a later Node timer turn; it is not a positive startup grace period.\n- Ordinary fallback polling is 16ms with missStreak=0.\n- After a failed acquisition increments missStreak (line 583), nonurgent retries use min(16 \u003c\u003c min(missStreak,5),500): 32,64,128,256,500ms, then 500ms. This applies before the first successful frame too. Native event callbacks can render immediately rather than waiting for that retry timer.\n- With an external bridge, nonurgent scheduling skips GPU-not-ready, clean, held, hidden, minimized, occluded or suspended windows. There is no initial 16ms polling timer while GPU setup remains pending in that path.\n\nnative/src/window_pump.rs:214-242 derives the platform wait from uv_backend_timeout and arms an I/O watcher to wake it. It adds no fixed sleep value. Previously captured native timestamps show a requested zero timeout can still spend 58-106ms inside winit's AppKit pass; this is distinct from the JS retry timer.\n\nOther SDK timers: renderer resource trim after 1000ms quiet, scheduled inside painting; animation motionWake at its computed deadline (minimum requested 1ms), also scheduled inside painting; caret blink 530ms; scrollbar linger 900ms and fade ticks 16ms over 250ms. CSS transition/animation delays are requested motion timing, not a global first-draw wait. Wiki HUD samples every 500ms. Screenshot tooling polls frame count every 100ms and waits another 2500ms before capture; ordinary wiki main does not use that script.\n\nThe relevant SDK manual delay is the surface retry backoff, which can add up to 500ms between attempts if native exposure events are absent. It explains the reduced attempt rate in recorded persistent stalls, not why the surface refuses frames for 60 seconds. Keep the native pump/JS callback delay and the test-only overall 60-second watchdog separate from this backoff.","files":null}]}